                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#examples_3"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#contrast_to_horizontal_categorification"} Contrast to horizontal categorification
              %li
                %a{:href => "#homotopification_versus_laxification"} Homotopification versus laxification
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Roughly speaking,
            %em vertical categorification
            is a procedure in which structures are generalized from the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            or from category theory to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %p What precisely that means may depend on circumstances and authors, to some extent. The following lists some common procedures that are known as categorification. They are in general different but may in cases lead to the same categorified notions, as discussed in the examples.
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification+in+representation+theory"} categorification in representation theory
          %h2#variants Variants
          %h3#as_a_section_of_decategorification As a section of decategorification
          %p
            What has a more specific definition is the process of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} k-morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            to produce a
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \lt n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (the set of isomorphism classes or equivalence classes of objects) when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
          %p
            One may understand
            %em vertical categorification
            as any operation that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            operation, and many examples in the literature are of this kind.
          %h4#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The maybe archetypical example of categorification as something that is taken by decategorification to the identity operation is the categorification of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
                of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
              %p
                The set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-category"} 0-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinSet
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} isomorphism classes
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinSet
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                however are in canonical bijection with the elements of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi FinSet
                        %mo ∼
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \simeq FinSet_\sim .
              %p
                So
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinSet
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                and accordingly
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinSet
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                is a (vertical) categorification of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              %p
                From the point of view of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi FinSet
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                %strong counting
                is about this. This point is made nicely in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/9802029"} BaDo98
              %p
                But there are other categorifications of the natural numbers, for instance the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
                (over any given
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
                is also canonically in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
                with the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≃
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi FinDimVect
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo ∼
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \;\simeq\; FinDimVect_{/\sim} \,.
          %h3#as_internalization_in_
            As internalization in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Cat
          %p
            Common structures such as
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            (the 2-morphisms then are the identities required to hold between the given morphisms).
          %p
            For instance a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            is an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            equipped with a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu : G \times G \to G
            and so on, and equipped with a 2-morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \circ Id_G \times \mu
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \circ \mu \times Id_G
          %p
            In such diagrammatic incarnation, these definitions may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalized
            into other categories. For instance a group internal to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
          %p
            But similarly one can also internalize in categories of higher categories. Let
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            be the category of (small) categories, regarded as an ordinary category. Then a group internal to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-group
          %p
            And under the decategorification functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat \to Set
            every categorified group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{G}
            in this sense maps to an ordinary group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and one could speak of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{G}
            being “a categorification” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Not every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-group
            that is a group object internal to the 1-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            is a
            %em weak
            group object in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            all 2-morphisms in the diagrammatic description of the concept of group necessarily had to be identities, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            they could be taken to be non-trivial. But if they are, one will usually want 3-morphisms (which now are necessarily identities) to relate various combinations of these 2-morphisms.
          %p
            One speaks of this process of categorification using weak internalization of diagrammatic descriptions as categorification of structures
            %em up to coherent higher equivalences
            or
            %em up to coherent higher homotopies
            \. One way to make this systematic is discussed below.
          %h4#examples_2 Examples
          %p In the sense of “weakly internalizing in a higher categorical context” we have the following examples of categorification:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the categorification of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            %li
              %p
                accordingly, the categorification of (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"}> sheaf
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                is (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"}> stack
                \-)2-topos, …
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                using these
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                notions one can then speak of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-space"} ∞-space
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                Since
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorial
                structures, many simplicial objects may be regarded as secretly being objects in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                = succeed "es," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    strict
                    = succeed "-groups" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn ∞
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
                it follows then that many objects in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                such as
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                may be regarded as being secretly about “categorified linear algebra” in some sense. For instance the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
                of
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
                of “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                \-vector spaces”. Notably
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} L-∞-algebra
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                = succeed "-vector" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                When understanding higher linear algebra in this sense, there are important
                = succeed "-categorifications" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transform"} Fourier-Mukai transform
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transform"} integral transform
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+%E2%88%9E-function+theory"} geometric ∞-function theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands"} geometric Langlands
                duality fits into this context.
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "Gram-Schmidt+process#CategorifiedGramSchmidtProcess"} categorified Gram-Schmidt process
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"}> (∞,1)-
                categorification of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential calculus
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie+calculus"} Goodwillie calculus
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"}> (∞,1)-
                categorification of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie+derivative"} Goodwillie derivative
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"}> (∞,1)-
                categorification of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+rule"} chain rule
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie+chain+rule"} Goodwillie chain rule
              %p etc.
          %h3#as_homotopy_coherent_resolution As homotopy coherent resolution
          %p The above process of categorification by coherently weak internalization into higher categorical contexts can be made systematic at least in some cases.
          %p
            If the structures being defined are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebras over
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            one may think of regarding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            as an
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} (∞,1)-operad
            = succeed "-operad." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %h4#examples_3 Examples
          %p For instance this way
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the notion of ordinary associative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                is sent to that of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} algebra
            %li
              %p
                the notion of ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                to
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} algebra
          %h2#contrast_to_horizontal_categorification Contrast to horizontal categorification
          %p
            Some people also speak of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} horizontal categorification
            as categorification. This is to be distinguished from vertical categorification.
          %p
            Some people just say ‘oidification’ for horizontal categorification, in which case it is consistent to speak of vertical categorificaton as just
            %em categorification
            \.
          %h2#homotopification_versus_laxification Homotopification versus laxification
          %p (Vertical) categorification can often be usefully decomposed into two operations.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong groupoidal categorification
                %strong homotopification
                or
                %strong groupoidification
                (although the latter term also has a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} different meaning
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
                (hence the name “homotopification”).
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong directed categorification
                %strong directification
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong laxification
          %p
            If you like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+thinking"} negative thinking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            by
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            by sets, especially the truth values of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equations
            %em they
            are the same are the same way, etc).
          %p
            However, when we apply “replace truth values by sets” to the truth values of the equality relation of a set, we end up with a groupoid, since the equality of a set is symmetric. Thus, while two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            of a set simply may (or may not) be equal, two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            in many different ways. And while two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+morphisms"} parallel isomorphisms
            in a groupoid may be equal, two parallel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences"} equivalences
            in a
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            To get from groupoids to categories, we need to also allow things which were previously invertible to be noninvertible, i.e. perform “directification.” We could also do this first starting from a set, obtaining a poset (in which the symmetric relation “is equal to” has been replaced by the non-symmetric one “is less than or equal to”). Then when we homotopify a poset, we get a category: while one element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            may precede an element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            from one object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of a category to an object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p
            This can also be understood naturally in the language of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-categories"} (n,r)-categories
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,r)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            in which all cells above dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            are invertible, and all cells above dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            are trivial. Thus, groupoidal categorification can be understood as increasing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            but keeping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            constant, while directification can be understood as increasing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            but keeping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            constant.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categorification"} (∞,1)-categorification
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification+in+representation+theory"} categorification in representation theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorified+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} categorified Dold-Kan correspondence
          %h2#references References
          %p The terminology goes back to
          %ul
            %li#CraneYetter96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Crane"}> Louis Crane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Yetter"} David Yetter
              %em Examples of categorification
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9607028"}> arXiv:q-alg/9607028
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p and was further amplified in
          %ul
            %li#BaezDolan98
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} James Dolan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categorification
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher Category Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 230
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/9802029"} arXiv:math.QA/9802029
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/10/what_is_categorification.html"} What is categorification?
          %p Lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alistair+Savage"}> Alistair Savage
                ,
                %em Introduction to categorification
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.6037"}> arXiv:1401.6037
                , slides:
                %a{:href => "https://alistairsavage.ca/talks/2014-savage-intro-to-categorification.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Dylan Madden,
                %em Introductory Categorification
                (2015) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/maths/people/staff/madden/1102171m.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (elementary introduction to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                  leading up to the example of the categorified
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+algebra"} Weyl algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Khovanov"} Mikhail Khovanov
                (notes by
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/You+Qi"} You Qi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to categorification
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.columbia.edu/~khovanov/cat2020/"} web
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://you-qi2121.github.io/mypage/categorificationnotes.html"} web
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/wdesax1c8il6tgwbi20t4/KhovanovYouQi-Categorification.pdf?rlkey=l5cm3khnzu604ijdnl06o89od&dl=0"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (focus on
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+homology"} link homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Ximenes+Martins"}> Yuri Ximenes Martins
                , part II of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical and Geometrical Methods in Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://repositorio.ufmg.br/bitstream/1843/32053/1/dissertacao_yuri_FINAL.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://math-phys.group/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/slides_yuri.pdf"} slides
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Ximenes+Martins"}> Yuri Ximenes Martins
                , Chapter 4 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introdução à Teoria da Homotopia Abstrata
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.05302"} arXiv:2008.05302
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-02908896v1"} hal-02908896v1
          %p
            A general notion of categorification for structures defined by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monads"} cartesian monads
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            in the sense of Leinster, can be found here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+Bourn"}> D. Bourn
              , J. Penon,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Catégorification de structures définies par monade cartésienne
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 46
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/numdam-bin/fitem?id=CTGDC_2005__46_1_2_0"} numdam
